insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('cy-f-099','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is JA3/JA3S fingerprinting?$$,$$A technique for fingerprinting TLS client/server handshake parameters to identify specific applications or malware families communicating over encrypted channels, without decrypting the traffic.$$),
('cy-f-100','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a SOC playbook maturity model (ad hoc to optimized)?$$,$$Describes how formalized incident response is: ad hoc (tribal knowledge) -> documented -> standardized -> measured -> optimized (continuously improved via metrics).$$),
('cy-f-101','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is pivoting across the Diamond Model's vertices?$$,$$Analysts pivot across the four vertices (e.g., from a known malware Capability to find related Infrastructure) to uncover the full scope of a campaign from a single known data point.$$),
('cy-f-102','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is a false flag operation in threat intelligence?$$,$$An attacker deliberately plants misleading indicators (language, tools, infrastructure) to make attribution point to a different threat actor or nation.$$),
('cy-f-103','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a hypothesis in threat hunting and where does it come from?$$,$$A specific, testable statement about possible undetected compromise (e.g., "an attacker is using WMI for lateral movement"), often derived from threat intel, red team results, or ATT&CK gaps.$$),
('cy-f-104','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is process injection and why is it hard to detect?$$,$$Malicious code runs inside a legitimate process's memory space, inheriting its trust and evading detections that only look at what's on disk or what process launched.$$),
('cy-f-105','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What does encrypted traffic analysis rely on since payloads can't be read?$$,$$Metadata: packet timing, size patterns, certificate details, and JA3-style fingerprints - detecting anomalies without decryption.$$),
('cy-f-106','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a cloud-native SIEM's advantage over traditional on-prem SIEM for cloud workloads?$$,$$Native integration with cloud provider APIs and logs (e.g., CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log) without needing agents or log forwarders on ephemeral resources.$$),
('cy-f-107','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is dwell time and why does it matter?$$,$$The duration between initial compromise and detection; shorter dwell time limits attacker opportunity to achieve objectives - a key SOC performance metric.$$),
('cy-f-108','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is beaconing jitter and why do attackers use it?$$,$$Randomizing the interval between C2 check-ins so traffic doesn't look perfectly periodic, evading simple beaconing-detection rules based on fixed timing.$$),
('cy-f-109','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is "pivoting" in threat hunting, as distinct from an attacker's network pivoting?$$,$$Starting from one known indicator (an IP, a hash) and querying related data sources to expand the investigation outward - not to be confused with an attacker pivoting through compromised hosts.$$),
('cy-f-110','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a false negative's cost compared to a false positive in detection engineering?$$,$$A false positive wastes analyst time; a false negative means a real attack goes completely undetected - asymmetric risk that shapes how aggressively rules are tuned.$$),
('cy-f-111','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is CVSS Environmental score used for?$$,$$Lets an organization adjust the base score for their specific context (asset criticality, existing compensating controls) - the final, most relevant severity number for prioritization.$$),
('cy-f-112','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is vulnerability aging and why track it?$$,$$How long a known vulnerability has remained unpatched; aging vulnerabilities indicate process failures and rising risk the longer they're exposed.$$),
('cy-f-113','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is a scan window and why does it matter operationally?$$,$$The scheduled time period during which vulnerability scans run - timed to avoid business-hours disruption, especially for active scans against sensitive/fragile systems.$$),
('cy-f-114','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is risk acceptance documentation and who signs it?$$,$$A record that a business risk owner formally acknowledges and accepts an unremediated vulnerability's risk, typically requiring their signature/approval, not the security team's.$$),
('cy-f-115','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is dependency confusion in software supply chain attacks?$$,$$Publishing a malicious package with the same name as an internal private package to a public registry, tricking build systems into pulling the attacker's version instead.$$),
('cy-f-116','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a business logic vulnerability?$$,$$A flaw in the application's intended workflow (e.g., applying a discount code unlimited times) rather than a coding bug - not caught by traditional scanners, found through manual testing.$$),
('cy-f-117','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the difference between a vulnerability's likelihood and its severity in prioritization?$$,$$Severity (CVSS) measures how bad exploitation would be; likelihood factors in exploitability, exposure, and active exploitation - both matter together, not just severity alone.$$),
('cy-f-118','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is scan authentication scope and why must it be maintained?$$,$$Credentialed scans need valid, current credentials across target systems; expired or narrow-scope credentials silently degrade scan accuracy without an obvious error.$$),
('cy-f-119','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is XML External Entity (XXE) injection?$$,$$Exploiting XML parsers that process external entity references to read local files, perform SSRF, or cause denial of service via maliciously crafted XML input.$$),
('cy-f-120','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)?$$,$$A formal inventory of all components/dependencies in a piece of software, enabling rapid identification of exposure when a new vulnerability is disclosed in a widely-used library.$$),
('cy-f-121','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is remediation SLA breach reporting used for?$$,$$Tracking and escalating vulnerabilities that exceeded their committed fix timeframe - surfaces systemic remediation bottlenecks to leadership.$$),
('cy-f-122','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the difference between eradication and remediation in IR?$$,$$Eradication removes the immediate threat (malware, attacker accounts) from affected systems; remediation addresses the underlying vulnerability/process gap so the SAME issue can't recur elsewhere.$$),
('cy-f-123','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is an IR retainer with a third-party firm?$$,$$A pre-arranged contract with an incident response vendor guaranteeing rapid engagement during a real incident - avoids negotiating contracts while actively under attack.$$),
('cy-f-124','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is live response vs dead-box forensics?$$,$$Live response collects volatile evidence from a running system (memory, connections) before it's powered down; dead-box forensics examines an already-imaged, powered-off system.$$),
('cy-f-125','cysa','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a corrective action tracker in post-incident review?$$,$$A list of specific, owned, deadlined action items from lessons learned, tracked to completion - prevents the review from becoming a report nobody acts on.$$),
('cy-f-126','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is business email compromise (BEC) specific containment?$$,$$Beyond isolating a host: revoking OAuth tokens/app passwords, forcing password resets, reviewing mail forwarding/rules the attacker may have added, and checking for further compromised accounts.$$),
('cy-f-127','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the practical risk of using an outdated hash algorithm for evidence integrity?$$,$$Modern algorithms (SHA-256) are extremely low-risk; using an outdated algorithm (MD5) for evidence hashing is a real, avoidable weakness worth flagging in forensic procedure review.$$),
('cy-f-128','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the difference between an IR plan and an IR playbook?$$,$$The IR plan is the overall program (roles, phases, policy); playbooks are scenario-specific tactical guides (ransomware playbook, BEC playbook) that operationalize the plan for specific threat types.$$),
('cy-f-129','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a false positive rate metric used for in vulnerability reporting?$$,$$Tracks scanner/tool accuracy over time; a rising false positive rate erodes trust in findings and signals a need for tuning.$$),
('cy-f-130','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a post-incident executive briefing, distinct from the full technical report?$$,$$A short, business-framed summary (what happened, impact, cost, fix status) delivered promptly to leadership, separate from the detailed technical report that follows later.$$),
('cy-f-131','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is benchmark comparison in security reporting?$$,$$Comparing your organization's metrics (MTTR, patch compliance) against industry peers or frameworks to contextualize whether performance is actually good or just internally consistent.$$),
('cy-f-132','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a regulatory notification checklist used for?$$,$$Ensures all applicable notification obligations (which regulators, which timeline, which authority) are identified and tracked during incident response, since missing one has legal consequences.$$),
('cy-f-133','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$Why show a finding's "age" alongside its "severity" in a report?$$,$$Age shows how long a risk has been outstanding; severity shows how bad it is - an old, unaddressed medium can be as concerning as a new critical, and reports should show both dimensions.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('cy-q-101','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An analyst sees encrypted TLS traffic to an unusual external IP but cannot decrypt it. Which technique lets them fingerprint the client application generating this traffic without decryption?$q$,$q$["Full packet capture only","JA3 TLS fingerprinting","Nslookup","Port scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$JA3 fingerprints the TLS handshake parameters (cipher suites, extensions) to identify the client application generating a session, without needing to decrypt the payload.$q$),
('cy-q-102','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Malware injects its code into the memory space of a legitimate, already-running svchost.exe process to evade detection. What technique is this?$q$,$q$["Process injection","DNS tunneling","Rootkit installation","SQL injection"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Running malicious code inside a legitimate process's memory space is process injection - it inherits that process's trust and identity.$q$),
('cy-q-103','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$Why is process injection particularly hard for signature-based tools to detect?$q$,$q$["It never executes any code","It inherits the trust and identity of a legitimate, already-running process, so file-based scanning sees nothing new on disk","It only works offline","It requires physical access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Because the malicious code runs inside a trusted, already-running process rather than as a new file on disk, disk-based signature scanning has nothing new to flag.$q$),
('cy-q-104','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst pivots from a known malicious IP address (Infrastructure) to discover the malware family (Capability) that communicates with it, then further pivots to identify likely victims (Victim). Which model structures this analysis?$q$,$q$["Cyber Kill Chain","Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","OWASP Top 10","CVSS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Diamond Model's four vertices - adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim - are exactly what this analyst is pivoting across to build out the full picture of a campaign.$q$),
('cy-q-105','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$Threat intelligence attributes an intrusion to a specific nation-state based on code comments in a foreign language and infrastructure registered in that country - but investigators later determine these were deliberately planted. What is this called?$q$,$q$["A false positive","A false flag operation","A honeytoken","A zero-day"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deliberately planting misleading attribution evidence to point investigators toward a different actor is a false flag operation, complicating accurate attribution.$q$),
('cy-q-106','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A hunt team forms the hypothesis "an attacker may be using WMI for lateral movement" based on a recent threat intel report about a specific APT group. What informed this hypothesis?$q$,$q$["Random guessing","External threat intelligence about known adversary TTPs","A vulnerability scan result","A compliance requirement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Hunt hypotheses are commonly derived from threat intelligence about known adversary tactics, techniques, and procedures relevant to the organization.$q$),
('cy-q-107','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A SOC wants to detect C2 beaconing where the attacker randomizes check-in intervals specifically to defeat simple fixed-timing detection rules. What is this randomization called, and what detection approach still catches it?$q$,$q$["Jitter; statistical analysis of connection patterns over a longer window rather than fixed intervals","Jitter; there is no way to detect it","Beaconing; only signature matching works","Encryption; only DNS filtering works"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Jitter defeats rigid fixed-interval detection, but statistical/behavioral analysis over a wider time window can still surface the underlying periodic pattern.$q$),
('cy-q-108','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which cloud-native capability lets a SOC ingest AWS API activity logs directly without deploying agents to ephemeral compute resources?$q$,$q$["A traditional on-prem SIEM with manual log shipping only","Native integration with CloudTrail via a cloud-native SIEM/connector","Physical network taps","USB log collection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Cloud-native SIEM integrations pull directly from provider APIs (CloudTrail), avoiding the need for agents on short-lived, auto-scaling resources.$q$),
('cy-q-109','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A SOC reduces average dwell time from 200 days to 12 days after deploying new detection capabilities. What does this metric directly measure?$q$,$q$["Time between initial compromise and detection","Time to patch a vulnerability","Time to write a report","Time between two backups"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Dwell time is specifically the gap between initial compromise and detection - a core measure of detection program effectiveness.$q$),
('cy-q-110','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Why does reducing dwell time matter operationally?$q$,$q$["It has no operational impact","Shorter dwell time limits how much damage/lateral movement an attacker can achieve before being caught","It only affects compliance paperwork","It increases attacker access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The longer an attacker goes undetected, the more time they have to move laterally, escalate privileges, and achieve their objectives - shrinking dwell time directly limits that opportunity.$q$),
('cy-q-111','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$A detection engineer is deciding how aggressively to tune a new rule. Which asymmetry should weigh most heavily in that decision?$q$,$q$["False positives and false negatives cost the same, so tune however is convenient","A false negative means a real attack goes completely undetected, while a false positive only wastes analyst time - the two are not equally costly","False negatives are always preferable","Tuning has no real trade-offs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A missed real attack (false negative) is categorically worse than a wasted investigation (false positive) - this asymmetry should shape tuning decisions rather than treating both errors as equal.$q$),
('cy-q-112','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the difference between hunting "pivoting" and an attacker's network "pivoting"?$q$,$q$["They are identical activities","Hunt pivoting expands an investigation outward from one known indicator across data sources; attacker pivoting means moving laterally through compromised hosts to reach new targets","Only attackers pivot; analysts never do","Pivoting always refers to network movement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The same word describes two different activities: an analyst expanding investigation scope from a known indicator, versus an attacker using one compromised host to reach another.$q$),
('cy-q-113','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A SOC playbook program has moved from "tribal knowledge only" to documented, standardized, and now measures performance metrics to drive continuous improvement. Which maturity stage best fits?$q$,$q$["Ad hoc","Optimized","There is no such thing as playbook maturity","Undefined"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Using metrics to drive continuous improvement is the hallmark of the optimized stage - the most mature end of the ad-hoc-to-optimized maturity spectrum.$q$),
('cy-q-114','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which artifact would MOST directly help identify what specific application generated an encrypted outbound connection, without decrypting the session?$q$,$q$["The full plaintext payload","A JA3/JA3S fingerprint of the TLS handshake","The victim's antivirus logs alone","A password policy document"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$JA3/JA3S fingerprints the handshake itself, identifying the generating application/library without needing the plaintext payload at all.$q$),
('cy-q-115','cysa',1,'1.2','matching',2,$q$Match each threat intelligence concept to its description.$q$,$q${"left":["Diamond Model","Pyramid of Pain","False flag","Dwell time"],"right":["Links adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim","Ranks indicators by how costly they are for an attacker to change","Deliberately misleading attribution evidence","Time between compromise and detection"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each concept plays a distinct role in threat intelligence analysis: the Diamond Model structures intrusion analysis, the pyramid ranks indicator durability, false flags mislead attribution, and dwell time measures detection speed.$q$),
('cy-q-116','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An analyst notices a host's network connections show perfectly random, non-repeating intervals to the same external IP rather than a fixed pattern. What should the analyst suspect?$q$,$q$["Normal user browsing","Jittered C2 beaconing designed to evade simple periodicity detection","A firewall misconfiguration","A DNS misconfiguration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Randomized-but-persistent connections to the same external host, rather than truly organic browsing, is the signature of jittered beaconing designed to evade simple timing-based detection.$q$),
('cy-q-117','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which is a legitimate reason threat actors deploy false flag indicators?$q$,$q$["To make attribution point toward a different actor or nation, complicating response and retaliation decisions","False flags serve no strategic purpose","To improve their own detection capability","To comply with disclosure regulations"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Misdirecting attribution complicates defenders' and governments' response decisions - a real strategic incentive behind false flag operations.$q$),
('cy-q-118','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A hunt investigates a hypothesis and finds no supporting evidence after exhausting relevant telemetry. What is the correct interpretation?$q$,$q$["The hypothesis was worthless and hunting failed","A negative result still has value - it increases confidence that this specific TTP is not present, and the hypothesis can be documented for future reference","The SOC should be shut down","The analyst should fabricate a finding to justify the time spent"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A well-formed hunt that finds nothing is not a failure - it provides genuine (if negative) evidence and documents ground already covered for future hunts.$q$),
('cy-q-119','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which log source would MOST directly reveal that a legitimate process spawned an unexpected child process consistent with process injection?$q$,$q$["EDR process-tree telemetry","A physical badge log","A DNS zone file","An HR system export"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$EDR process-tree data shows parent-child relationships and anomalous behavior within a process, directly surfacing injection-related activity that other logs wouldn't capture.$q$),
('cy-q-120','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY reason encrypted traffic analysis relies on metadata (timing, size, certificate details) rather than payload content?$q$,$q$["Metadata is less accurate than payload","The payload is encrypted and unreadable without decryption capability, but metadata remains observable in transit","Metadata is illegal to collect","Payload analysis is always faster"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Without decryption, only metadata surrounding the encrypted session (timing, sizes, certificates) is observable - so detection must work with what's actually visible.$q$),
('cy-q-121','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which combination of indicators would a threat intel analyst treat with the MOST suspicion as a potential false flag?$q$,$q$["Consistent TTPs matching a known group's established pattern over years","Deliberately obvious, uncharacteristic artifacts (language, easily-found infrastructure) inconsistent with the sophistication of the rest of the intrusion","A group using the same infrastructure repeatedly across campaigns","Malware compiled in a timezone matching the group's known operating hours"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Artifacts that are conveniently obvious and inconsistent with an otherwise sophisticated intrusion are a classic tell that they were planted deliberately to mislead attribution.$q$),
('cy-q-122','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A SOC's cloud-native SIEM ingests CloudTrail, GuardDuty findings, and VPC Flow Logs automatically as new AWS accounts are onboarded. What operational benefit does this provide over a traditional on-prem SIEM approach for cloud workloads?$q$,$q$["No benefit - they are equivalent","Native API integration avoids deploying and maintaining agents on ephemeral, auto-scaling cloud resources","Cloud-native SIEMs cannot detect threats","On-prem SIEMs are always superior for cloud"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Direct API-based ingestion scales naturally with ephemeral cloud resources, unlike agent-based approaches that struggle to keep pace with auto-scaling infrastructure.$q$),
('cy-q-123','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$Which statement correctly ranks detection engineering priorities when tuning a new high-volume rule?$q$,$q$["Maximize alert volume regardless of accuracy","Balance catching true positives against minimizing false positives, since a false negative (missed real attack) is generally costlier than analyst time spent on a false positive","Disable all rules to avoid any false positives","Only tune rules once per year regardless of performance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Effective tuning weighs the cost asymmetry between false negatives and false positives, aiming to minimize missed real attacks while keeping noise manageable - not eliminating either error type blindly.$q$),
('cy-q-124','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Two vulnerabilities share the same CVSS Base score, but one sits on an internet-facing payment server and the other on an isolated lab machine. Which score adjusts for this organizational context?$q$,$q$["Temporal score","Environmental score","Base score only","There is no such adjustment"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Environmental metric group lets an organization adjust severity for its own asset criticality and compensating controls, unlike the fixed Base score.$q$),
('cy-q-125','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A critical vulnerability has remained unpatched for 180 days despite a 30-day SLA. What does tracking this vulnerability's "age" reveal?$q$,$q$["Nothing useful","A process failure and rising exposure the longer the vulnerability remains unaddressed","That the vulnerability is now safe","That CVSS scores decay automatically over time"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Tracking age surfaces exactly this kind of SLA breach, signaling both increasing risk exposure and an underlying process problem worth escalating.$q$),
('cy-q-126','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Why are vulnerability scans against fragile OT/ICS systems typically scheduled within a defined scan window rather than run continuously?$q$,$q$["Scan windows have no real purpose","To time disruptive active scanning for low-impact periods and avoid crashing fragile systems during business-critical hours","Scanning is illegal outside a window","Scan windows increase scan accuracy automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Scheduling active scans to low-impact windows minimizes the operational risk of disrupting fragile systems that can crash under aggressive probing.$q$),
('cy-q-127','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A business risk owner formally signs a document accepting a vulnerability's risk rather than remediating it. Who should this signature belong to?$q$,$q$["The security analyst who found it","The business owner accountable for the affected system/process","The vendor","No one - acceptance requires no documentation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Risk acceptance is a business decision, so accountability - and the signature - belongs to the business owner, not the security team that merely identified the risk.$q$),
('cy-q-128','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$An attacker publishes a malicious package to a public registry using the exact same name as a company's internal private package, and the company's build system pulls the attacker's version instead. What supply-chain attack is this?$q$,$q$["Typosquatting","Dependency confusion","SQL injection","Cross-site scripting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Exploiting a build system's package resolution to prefer a public, identically-named malicious package over the intended internal one is dependency confusion.$q$),
('cy-q-129','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A scanner reports no vulnerabilities in an e-commerce checkout flow, but manual testing reveals a discount code can be applied unlimited times due to a workflow flaw. What class of issue is this?$q$,$q$["A business logic vulnerability, typically missed by automated scanners","A buffer overflow","A zero-day","A firmware vulnerability"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Business logic flaws exploit the intended workflow rather than a coding bug, so automated scanners - which look for known patterns - routinely miss them.$q$),
('cy-q-130','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Two vulnerabilities have identical CVSS severity, but one is listed in CISA KEV with active exploitation and the other has zero known exploitation attempts. Which additionally-weighted factor explains why the KEV-listed one is prioritized first?$q$,$q$["Severity alone determines priority","Likelihood/exploitation activity, which combines with severity to drive real-world prioritization","CVSS scores change based on KEV listing","KEV listing has no bearing on prioritization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Real-world prioritization blends severity with exploitation likelihood - active exploitation (KEV) is a strong signal that outweighs equal severity alone.$q$),
('cy-q-131','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A credentialed scan begins reporting far more findings than usual after an IT change. Investigation reveals the scan account's credentials expired and the scan silently fell back to non-credentialed mode. What does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Credentialed scan accuracy silently degrades if scope/credentials aren't maintained, without necessarily throwing an obvious error","Scans always fail loudly when credentials expire","This has no impact on scan accuracy","Non-credentialed scans are always more accurate"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Scan accuracy can degrade silently when credentials lapse, producing misleadingly noisy results without an obvious failure signal - a real operational trap.$q$),
('cy-q-132','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$An attacker submits crafted XML containing a reference to an external entity, causing the server to read and return the contents of a local system file. What vulnerability is this?$q$,$q$["XML External Entity (XXE) injection","Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Directory traversal"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Exploiting XML parsers that resolve external entity references is XXE injection - it can read local files, perform SSRF, or cause denial of service.$q$),
('cy-q-133','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A newly disclosed critical vulnerability affects a widely used open-source library. Which artifact lets a company instantly determine every application that uses this library?$q$,$q$["A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)","A firewall rule set","A password policy","An org chart"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An SBOM inventories every component and dependency in software, enabling rapid exposure identification the moment a component-level vulnerability is disclosed.$q$),
('cy-q-134','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Leadership asks for a report showing which vulnerabilities have exceeded their committed remediation SLA. What is the purpose of this report?$q$,$q$["To publicly shame individual engineers","To surface systemic remediation bottlenecks so leadership can address root causes, not just individual findings","It has no operational purpose","To replace the risk register entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SLA breach reporting is a management tool for surfacing systemic process problems, not a mechanism for individual blame.$q$),
('cy-q-135','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$Which combination BEST represents comprehensive vulnerability prioritization, beyond CVSS base score alone?$q$,$q$["CVSS base score only","CVSS base score combined with exploitability (KEV/EPSS), exposure, and asset criticality","Alphabetical order of the CVE ID","Whichever vulnerability was found first"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Real prioritization blends multiple signals - base severity plus exploitation likelihood, exposure, and business criticality - rather than any single factor alone.$q$),
('cy-q-136','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Why might a security team choose PASSIVE scanning for a segment containing legacy medical devices?$q$,$q$["Passive scanning observes traffic without directly probing fragile devices, avoiding the disruption active scanning risks","Passive scanning is always more accurate","Active scanning is required by regulation for medical devices","There is no difference between active and passive scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Passive scanning only observes existing traffic, avoiding the probing that can crash fragile legacy medical devices - a safer trade-off for coverage versus depth.$q$),
('cy-q-137','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A CI/CD pipeline blocks a build because a dependency scan (SCA) flagged a critical CVE in a transitive dependency the developers didn't even know they were using. What made this visible?$q$,$q$["Manual code review only","Software Composition Analysis scanning the full dependency tree, including transitive/indirect dependencies","A firewall rule","A password audit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCA tools trace the entire dependency tree, surfacing risk from indirect dependencies that manual review would likely never catch.$q$),
('cy-q-138','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Which testing approach is MOST likely to uncover a business logic flaw that no automated scanner would flag?$q$,$q$["Manual penetration testing / abuse-case testing by a human analyst","An automated CVSS lookup","A firewall configuration review","A password complexity check"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Business logic flaws require human understanding of intended workflow versus actual exploitable behavior - something automated tools generally cannot evaluate.$q$),
('cy-q-139','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability cannot be patched due to a vendor dependency, so the team documents formal risk acceptance with a defined review date. Why include a review date rather than accepting indefinitely?$q$,$q$["Review dates are meaningless","Circumstances change - a vendor patch may become available, or the asset's exposure may increase - so acceptance should be periodically re-evaluated, not permanent","Indefinite acceptance is always safer","Documentation requires a random date"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Conditions change over time; a review date forces periodic reassessment rather than letting a risk acceptance quietly become permanent and stale.$q$),
('cy-q-140','cysa',2,'2.2','ordering',2,$q$Order these vulnerability prioritization inputs from LEAST to MOST specific to your organization's actual risk.$q$,$q$["CVSS Base score (generic, vendor-published)","CVSS Temporal score (adjusts for exploit maturity over time)","CVSS Environmental score (adjusts for your specific asset context)","Full risk-based prioritization combining CVSS, KEV/EPSS, exposure, and asset criticality"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Base score is generic and vendor-set; Temporal adds time-varying factors; Environmental tailors to your specific context; full risk-based prioritization synthesizes everything into the most organization-specific view.$q$),
('cy-q-141','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A scan of a cloud environment misses newly launched auto-scaling instances that existed for only 20 minutes before termination. What scanning approach would better cover ephemeral cloud assets?$q$,$q$["Scheduled weekly scans only","Continuous/agentless cloud-native scanning integrated with the cloud provider's asset inventory API","Quarterly manual scans","No scanning is possible for ephemeral assets"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Only continuous, API-integrated scanning can keep pace with resources that exist for minutes - scheduled or manual scans will systematically miss them.$q$),
('cy-q-142','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$What risk does an unscanned base container image pulled from a public registry introduce, even if your own application code has zero vulnerabilities?$q$,$q$["No risk - only your own code matters","The base image's own known vulnerabilities are inherited by every container built on top of it","Base images cannot contain vulnerabilities","Public registries scan all images for you automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Every container inherits whatever flaws exist in its base image - your own code being clean doesn't protect against a vulnerable foundation layer.$q$),
('cy-q-143','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Which OWASP-style vulnerability involves an application accepting a user-supplied URL and fetching it server-side, potentially reaching internal-only resources?$q$,$q$["Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)","Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Insecure deserialization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSRF coerces the server itself into making attacker-chosen requests, which can reach internal resources the attacker couldn't otherwise access directly.$q$),
('cy-q-144','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability management program's remediation SLA compliance has dropped from 95% to 60% over two quarters. Which action is MOST appropriate for a reporting analyst to take?$q$,$q$["Hide the trend from leadership","Surface the declining trend with likely root causes (headcount, process, tooling) so leadership can address the systemic issue","Delete historical data to reset the metric","Ignore it since 60% is still a majority"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A significant declining trend deserves proactive escalation with context - hiding, deleting, or dismissing it all leave a real operational problem unaddressed.$q$),
('cy-q-145','cysa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$After eradicating malware from an infected host, the team also patches the vulnerability it exploited and retrains users who fell for the phishing lure. What is the PATCHING and TRAINING part of this response called, distinct from removing the malware itself?$q$,$q$["Eradication","Remediation - addressing the underlying cause so the same class of incident doesn't recur","Containment","Detection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Removing the malware is eradication; fixing the vulnerability and training users addresses the underlying cause - that's remediation, aimed at preventing recurrence.$q$),
('cy-q-146','cysa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A company signs a contract with an external IR firm guaranteeing a 1-hour response SLA if a major incident occurs, negotiated well before any incident happens. What is this called?$q$,$q$["An IR retainer","A risk register","A BIA","A change request"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A pre-arranged contract guaranteeing rapid IR engagement is a retainer, secured proactively rather than during an active crisis.$q$),
('cy-q-147','cysa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Why do organizations negotiate IR retainers in advance rather than seeking a firm during an active incident?$q$,$q$["Retainers have no real benefit","Negotiating contracts and vetting a vendor takes time an organization doesn't have while actively under attack","Retainers are required by law everywhere","Active incidents make negotiation easier"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Contract negotiation and vendor vetting take time that's simply unavailable mid-crisis - retainers move that overhead to a calmer moment.$q$),
('cy-q-148','cysa',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A responder captures RAM and active network connections from a running compromised server BEFORE powering it down for imaging. What is this called?$q$,$q$["Dead-box forensics","Live response","Data carving","Chain of custody"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Collecting volatile evidence from a still-running system before shutdown is live response, capturing data that would otherwise be lost.$q$),
('cy-q-149','cysa',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$After a system is already powered off, an investigator examines a forensic image of its disk. What is this called, as distinct from live response?$q$,$q$["Dead-box forensics","Live response","Threat hunting","Vulnerability scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Examining an already-imaged, powered-off system is dead-box forensics - the counterpart to live response's volatile-evidence collection.$q$),
('cy-q-150','cysa',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A lessons-learned meeting produces a list of five specific improvements, each assigned an owner and a deadline, tracked until completion. What does this tracker prevent?$q$,$q$["Nothing - the meeting itself is sufficient","The review becoming a report that documents good intentions but drives no actual change","The need for any future incidents","Faster detection automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Without owner/deadline tracking, lessons-learned findings routinely go unimplemented - the tracker is what turns intentions into actual improvement.$q$),
('cy-q-151','cysa',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$During a business email compromise incident, the attacker set up hidden mail-forwarding rules and used stolen OAuth tokens. Beyond resetting the compromised password, what MUST containment also address?$q$,$q$["Nothing else is needed after a password reset","Revoking OAuth tokens/app passwords and reviewing/removing malicious mail rules, since a password reset alone doesn't invalidate those","Only isolating the network","Disabling all company email accounts"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A password reset doesn't automatically revoke standing OAuth grants or remove mail rules the attacker configured - both must be explicitly addressed for real containment.$q$),
('cy-q-152','cysa',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Why would using MD5 instead of SHA-256 for evidence hashing be flagged as a weakness during a forensic procedure review?$q$,$q$["MD5 is always faster and equally secure","MD5 has known collision weaknesses that could theoretically undermine confidence in evidence integrity, unlike SHA-256","Hash algorithm choice never matters for evidence","SHA-256 cannot be used for forensic hashing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$MD5's known collision vulnerabilities are a real (if practically low-risk) weakness that a rigorous procedure review would flag in favor of a stronger algorithm like SHA-256.$q$),
('cy-q-153','cysa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A company maintains a general Incident Response Plan describing roles and phases, plus a separate detailed Ransomware Playbook with step-by-step tactical actions. What is the relationship between these two documents?$q$,$q$["They are identical documents with different names","The IR plan is the overall program; the playbook operationalizes it with scenario-specific tactical steps for a particular threat type","Playbooks replace the need for an IR plan","IR plans are only used for ransomware"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The plan sets the overall program structure; playbooks translate that structure into concrete, scenario-specific actions for particular threat types.$q$),
('cy-q-154','cysa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which containment action is MOST appropriate as an IMMEDIATE first step when ransomware is actively spreading between file servers?$q$,$q$["Schedule a lessons-learned meeting","Isolate affected hosts/segments from the network immediately to stop the spread","Wait for the next scheduled maintenance window","Notify the media"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$With active spread underway, immediate isolation is the priority - every other action (review, notification) follows once the bleeding stops.$q$),
('cy-q-155','cysa',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why is root cause analysis performed AFTER containment and eradication rather than during the active response?$q$,$q$["RCA should replace containment entirely","Active response prioritizes stopping ongoing damage; deep root-cause analysis happens once the immediate threat is controlled, without that time pressure","RCA is never necessary","Containment should wait for RCA to finish first"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Stopping active damage takes priority under time pressure; thorough root-cause analysis needs the calmer conditions that come after the immediate threat is controlled.$q$),
('cy-q-156','cysa',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A responder images a drive but forgets to hash the source media before imaging, only hashing the resulting image. What weakness does this create?$q$,$q$["None - hashing only the image is always sufficient","Without a source hash, there's no way to cryptographically prove the image is an unaltered, faithful copy of the original source media","Hashing is unnecessary for legal proceedings","This actually strengthens the evidence"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Proving a faithful copy requires comparing source and image hashes - hashing only the image leaves no baseline to verify against, weakening the evidentiary claim.$q$),
('cy-q-157','cysa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY reason a mature SOC defines specific playbooks for common incident types (phishing, ransomware, BEC) rather than relying on general IR plan guidance alone?$q$,$q$["Playbooks slow down response unnecessarily","Scenario-specific tactical steps reduce decision time and ensure consistent, complete response under the pressure of an active incident","General guidance is always sufficient","Playbooks are only useful for training, never real incidents"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Tactical playbooks remove ambiguity exactly when responders have the least time to figure things out - ensuring a consistent, complete response under pressure.$q$),
('cy-q-158','cysa',3,'3.2','ordering',2,$q$Order these BEC-specific containment steps after initial detection.$q$,$q$["Reset the compromised account's password","Revoke active sessions and OAuth tokens/app passwords","Review and remove malicious mail-forwarding rules","Check for additional compromised accounts using similar patterns"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Reset the immediate credential first, then invalidate any standing access tokens a password reset alone wouldn't kill, clean up attacker-configured mail rules, then scope whether other accounts were also compromised.$q$),
('cy-q-159','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability scanner's false positive rate rises from 5% to 25% over six months. What does tracking this metric enable?$q$,$q$["Nothing actionable","Recognizing degrading tool/rule accuracy early enough to retune before it erodes analyst trust in findings","It has no relationship to trust in the tool","False positive rate cannot be measured"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Watching this trend catches degrading accuracy early, before analysts start distrusting or ignoring the tool's output entirely.$q$),
('cy-q-160','cysa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Immediately after containing a major incident, the CISO sends a one-page summary of impact, cost estimate, and current fix status to the executive team, with the full technical report following days later. Why send the short version first?$q$,$q$["The full report is unnecessary","Leadership needs a fast, business-framed understanding to make immediate decisions, while the detailed technical report takes longer to compile accurately","Technical reports should always come first","Executive summaries replace the need for technical detail entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Leadership decisions (notification, PR, resourcing) can't wait for a fully verified technical report - a fast business-level summary serves that urgent need first.$q$),
('cy-q-161','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A report shows the organization's mean time to remediate critical vulnerabilities is 45 days, compared to an industry benchmark of 15 days. What does this comparison provide that the raw 45-day number alone does not?$q$,$q$["Nothing additional","Context - whether 45 days represents good, average, or concerning performance relative to peers","It replaces the need for the raw metric","Benchmarks are irrelevant to security reporting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A raw number means little without a reference point; benchmarking against peers or frameworks tells leadership whether 45 days is actually a problem.$q$),
('cy-q-162','cysa',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$An incident potentially triggers notification obligations to three different regulators with three different deadlines. What tool helps ensure none is missed?$q$,$q$["Guessing which regulator matters most","A regulatory notification checklist tracking every applicable obligation, authority, and deadline","Ignoring all but the largest regulator","Notifying only if a customer complains"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A structured checklist tracking every applicable obligation and deadline is the only reliable way to avoid missing one - the legal consequences of a miss are real.$q$),
('cy-q-163','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A finding has been open for 200 days and is rated "Medium" severity. Another finding opened yesterday is rated "Critical." Why might a well-designed report display BOTH severity and age together rather than sorting by severity alone?$q$,$q$["Age is irrelevant to risk","A long-neglected medium-severity finding represents accumulated, unaddressed risk that pure severity sorting would bury beneath every new critical","Severity always outweighs age in every case","Reports should never show more than one dimension"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Sorting by severity alone hides long-standing risk under a constant stream of new criticals; showing age too surfaces neglected findings that deserve attention.$q$),
('cy-q-164','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which metric is a true KPI rather than a raw metric?$q$,$q$["Total number of scans run","Percentage of critical vulnerabilities remediated within SLA","Number of employees in the security team","Number of servers in the data center"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A KPI ties a measurement to a goal and indicates performance - SLA compliance percentage does that; the others are simple counts with no built-in performance judgment.$q$),
('cy-q-165','cysa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Why should incident communication plans explicitly name a single spokesperson for external/media statements?$q$,$q$["It doesn't matter who speaks externally","Inconsistent or unauthorized statements to media/customers during a crisis can create legal exposure and conflicting information","Multiple simultaneous spokespeople always improve clarity","Media communication is never part of IR planning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A single designated spokesperson prevents conflicting or unauthorized statements from creating additional legal and reputational damage during an already stressful event.$q$),
('cy-q-166','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A report recommends "improve patch management" with no owner or deadline attached. What is missing that would make this actionable?$q$,$q$["Nothing is missing","Assignment of a specific owner and due date so the recommendation can actually be tracked to completion","The recommendation is already complete","Recommendations never need owners"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A recommendation without an owner and deadline has no accountability mechanism - it will likely never be tracked to actual completion.$q$),
('cy-q-167','cysa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Which stakeholder group specifically needs to know about evidence preservation and chain-of-custody requirements during an incident?$q$,$q$["Marketing only","Legal counsel, to advise on regulatory/litigation preservation obligations","Facilities management only","No one needs this information"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Legal counsel needs early visibility into preservation obligations to advise on litigation holds and regulatory requirements tied to the evidence being handled.$q$),
('cy-q-168','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$A CISO wants a single number combining a vulnerability's severity, its exposure, and the criticality of the affected asset to prioritize the weekly remediation queue. What should the report provide?$q$,$q$["The raw CVE ID alone","A composite risk score blending severity, exposure, and asset criticality","Only the CVSS base score","Alphabetical sorting of vulnerability names"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A composite risk score is exactly designed to blend multiple prioritization factors into one number stakeholders can act on and track over time.$q$),
('cy-q-169','cysa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Why is a blameless tone in an incident report more likely to produce an accurate account of what happened?$q$,$q$["Blame has no effect on reporting accuracy","People are more forthcoming with complete, honest details when they aren't afraid of being individually blamed, surfacing the true root cause","Blameless reports are required by law","Blame always improves the quality of reporting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fear of blame causes people to omit or soften details; removing that fear gets the honest, complete account needed to find the actual root cause.$q$),
('cy-q-170','cysa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which reporting practice most directly closes the loop between "we found a problem" and "the problem is actually fixed"?$q$,$q$["Publishing the finding once with no further follow-up","Action item tracking - assigning owners and deadlines, and following each finding to verified closure","Deleting old findings after 30 days","Reporting only successes, never open issues"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Tracking each finding to verified closure, with clear ownership and deadlines, is what actually turns a reported problem into a fixed one - reporting alone changes nothing.$q$);
